course: dictionary
lesson: 7798
title: A quarrel is an angry argument
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A quarrel is an angry argument between people who disagree, often friends or family. As a verb, to quarrel means to have that argument. The ending tells you which one you are reading: quarrels is a noun or a verb, depending on the sentence.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «They often quarrelled, like neighbouring schools with their own rules and uniforms, but they shared a language.» Quarrelled is the past tense, so the sentence says they had arguments in the past. The word «but» tells you they still had things in common.
explain: A quarrel is usually settled with words, not hands. «Most quarrels are settled by talking, deals and courts; war comes when talking fails.» So if talking works, the quarrel ends. Common phrases to notice are have a quarrel, quarrel with someone, quarrel about something, and make up after a quarrel.
fact: A quarrel is an angry argument between people who disagree, often friends or family.
fact: To quarrel means to have an angry argument with someone.
fact: «Estranged» means not in contact any more after a quarrel.
fact: To have no quarrel with something means you do not disagree with it.
exercise: «They often quarrelled, like neighbouring schools with their own rules, but they shared a language.» What does quarrelled mean here? | Built a castle together | Won a prize | *Had angry arguments | Shared a uniform
exercise: Mia says, «I have no quarrel with the plan.» What does she mean? | She wants to argue about the plan | She has lost the plan | *She does not disagree with the plan | She is angry about the plan
exercise: «Most quarrels are settled by talking, deals and courts.» What settles most quarrels? | *Talking and making deals | Forgetting who was right | Starting a war | Using force with hands
test: Which word is the verb in «The twins quarrel every morning about the toast»? | *quarrel | morning | every | twins
test: Jun and Sam had a quarrel, then made up. What does «made up» mean here? | They bought a new toy | *They became friends again | They started a new argument | They moved to another school
exam: Read «Most quarrels are settled by talking, deals and courts.» Which word is closest in meaning to settled here? | Hidden | Started | *Solved | Forgotten
